        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>When to Use Temporary Disposable Email</h2>
  <p>
    Use temporary email when you won't need the account or the messages later:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Software testing:</strong> checking signup, password-reset and confirmation emails without filling your own inbox with test accounts.</li>
    <li><strong>Gated downloads:</strong> getting a whitepaper, case study or template without signing up for the sales emails that follow.</li>
    <li><strong>One-off verification:</strong> receiving a single code or activation link without cluttering your main inbox. (<a href="/blog/understanding-otp-verification-codes/">Learn how OTP extraction works</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Sites you don't trust:</strong> forums, Wi-Fi sign-in pages and other places that collect addresses for marketing.</li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote>
    A temporary inbox keeps one-off mail completely separate from your real one. When you're done with it, you delete it.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>When to Use Email Aliases</h2>
  <p>
    Use an alias when you'll keep hearing from the service but still want to hide your real address:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Online Shopping and E-Commerce:</strong> Receiving tracking numbers, return authorizations, and warranty documentation over several weeks or months.</li>
    <li><strong>Paid subscriptions:</strong> software you pay for, where you may need a password reset or a receipt next year.</li>
    <li><strong>Newsletters you want:</strong> publications you actually read, with the option to switch off that alias if the sender starts spamming you.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Using Both</h2>
  <p>
    Most people get the best results by combining them:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>High trust:</strong> your real address, only for banks, healthcare, work and people you know.</li>
    <li><strong>Medium trust (ongoing):</strong> an alias for each shop, streaming service or community you'll stay with.</li>
    <li><strong>Low trust (one-off):</strong> <a href="/">TempoEmails</a> for quick signups, downloads, short trials and sites you don't know.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Conclusion</h2>
  <p>
    Temporary email is for things you'll forget about tomorrow; aliases are for things you'll keep. Used together, they keep spam out of your main inbox and limit how far a leak or a tracker can follow you.
  </p>
